# DTF tier manifest: db-pool-load-shed  (artifact-keeper#4182, 1.13.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem + limits.db-pool (passes the pool sizing and
# admission knobs through to the backend).
#
# THE PROBLEM (#4182)
# Under a burst (a fleet of package installers, each with dozens of fetches in
# flight) every request needs a pooled database connection, and the pool has
# DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS of them. A request that found none queued on the
# pool until DATABASE_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_SECS (5 s) ran out and only then got
# 503 POOL_EXHAUSTED: every overflow request stalled for the whole timeout
# before it was told to back off. /readyz ran its SELECT 1 on the same pool,
# so under the same burst it waited out the timeout too and answered 503
# not_ready: the orchestrator would take a replica that is busy, not broken,
# out of service and move its load onto the others.
#
# THE FIX (1.13.0)
#   * a router-wide admission gate, before any auth or handler work: at most
#     DATABASE_REQUEST_CONCURRENCY requests run their handler phase while the
#     pool is busy; past it a request queues at most
#     DATABASE_REQUEST_ACQUIRE_WAIT_MS for a slot (or is shed at once when
#     the predicted backlog is longer) and is answered the same
#     503 POOL_EXHAUSTED + Retry-After, counted in ak_db_requests_shed_total;
#   * /health and /readyz read the database through their own one-connection
#     pool (DATABASE_PROBE_CONNECTIONS=1), so a saturated request pool does
#     not fail them.
#
# THE PRIMITIVE: A DETERMINISTICALLY SATURATED POOL
# The backend runs with DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=4 and
# DATABASE_REQUEST_CONCURRENCY=4 (the shipped default is four slots per
# connection; one per connection makes "every slot held" reachable with a
# handful of requests) and DATABASE_REQUEST_ACQUIRE_WAIT_MS=500 (shipped
# default 2000; 500 keeps the fast-vs-5 s split wide). Main ignores both. The oracle opens its own
# psql session in this slot's Postgres container that takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE
# on `repositories` and sleeps, then issues 6 admin GET /api/v1/repositories
# ("slow requests"): each takes a pooled connection and blocks on the lock,
# so all 4 connections (and, on the fix, all 4 admission slots) are held and
# nothing frees one until the oracle ends
# the locking session. Every probe below runs in that window, then the
# oracle terminates the session and checks recovery. Only this slot's own
# Postgres (DB_CONTAINER) is touched; an EXIT trap ends the lock session if
# the oracle dies mid-window.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL) before the window: /readyz 200, the anonymous download of a
#             public generic artifact 200 + bytes, the admin repository list
#             200. During the window: an anonymous request to an admin
#             endpoint and a wrong Basic password are never 2xx (a shed is a
#             503, it never lets a request through). After the window: the
#             download, the repository list and /readyz succeed again, a burst
#             of 20 parallel downloads all answer 200, and the slow requests
#             that held the connections complete.
#   (FIX #4182) during the window the overflow request (the anonymous
#             download) answers 503 {code: POOL_EXHAUSTED} + a Retry-After
#             of whole seconds in under 2.5 s. RED on main: the same 503 only after the 5 s
#             acquire timeout.
#   (FIX #4182) during the window /readyz answers 200 ready (database
#             healthy). RED on main: 503 not_ready, "Database unreachable:
#             pool timed out".
#   (FIX #4182) after the window ak_db_requests_shed_total on
#             /api/v1/admin/metrics is at least 1. RED on main: no such
#             series.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false so a 429 can never masquerade as either verdict.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem limits.db-pool"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
COVERS="4182"
export DATABASE_MAX_CONNECTIONS="4"
export DATABASE_MIN_CONNECTIONS="0"
export DATABASE_REQUEST_CONCURRENCY="4"
export DATABASE_REQUEST_ACQUIRE_WAIT_MS="500"
